  "worked_solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-1}{3-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of $-2$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$ To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-2(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =-2x \\\\ y & =-2x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
